+# GPT response
+
+1. **Build two tiers first.** Use short BP → unchanged per-basis Relay-BP. Compare a deterministic convergence gate against an ML acceptance gate. This isolates whether ML adds value beyond early stopping. Defer full-XYZ decoding, partial corrections, and loss until this comparison works. The 68k-column model adds substantial integration and runtime uncertainty.
+
+   A middle tier earns its place only if it cheaply solves shots rejected by the light stage with sufficiently few additional logical failures. Benchmark one candidate, BP-OSD order 0, on that rejected subset. Add BP-LSD only if time remains. Do not assume a decoder that is fast unconditionally is fast on this selected, difficult population. Repeated BP work across tiers can erase savings; assume cold fallback unless actual state transfer is implemented.
+
+2. **Light stage: whole-shot acceptance.** Start with `BpDecoder(H, error_channel=priors, max_iter=20, bp_method="minimum_sum", ms_scaling_factor=1.0, schedule="parallel", omp_thread_count=1, input_vector_type="syndrome")`. Compare a 10-iteration cap in the pilot. Instantiate once per worker/basis/p.
+
+   Accept only if the returned candidate satisfies `H @ e % 2 == syndrome` **and** passes a validation-selected risk threshold. For the simplest ablation, accept every syndrome-consistent result. Consistency is necessary, but cannot certify the logical sector. Nonconvergence always falls back; an ML prediction must not make an inconsistent candidate acceptable.
+
+   Do not freeze “confident” mechanism bits. These are DEM mechanisms, not independent physical-qubit decisions; BP confidence is not a certificate. Freezing restricts subsequent solutions. Even reversible residual decoding requires tracking the committed logical frame and transforming the inference problem correctly. Pass the original syndrome and priors to fallback.
+
+3. **Router and labels.** Use syndrome weight, BP iteration count, convergence, residual weight, candidate weight, quantiles of absolute posterior LLRs, fraction of low-magnitude LLRs, and prior-weighted candidate cost. Include basis and p; normalize counts where appropriate. The installed API exposes `iter`, `converge`, and `log_prob_ratios`; do not promise iteration histories without instrumentation.
+
+   Start with standardized logistic regression; compare a shallow tree only if useful. Train on syndrome-consistent light outputs. Simulator observables provide free correctness labels: `(L @ e) % 2 != obs`. Relay is a comparator, never ground truth. Run Relay on a modest common sample to record both correctness and runtime; distinguish light-wrong/Relay-right from light-right/Relay-wrong. Oversampled failures need weighting and calibration on natural-prevalence data.
+
+   Optimize measured total mean latency subject to an upper confidence bound on **paired excess per-cycle LER** below a predeclared epsilon. Select thresholds on validation data, then lock them. Also report p95/p99 and fallback fraction. Initially constrain harmful substitutions conservatively rather than relying on rare “rescues” to cancel harm. Hard cases may still dominate p99: routing easy shots away from Relay does not accelerate the hardest fallbacks.
+
+4. **Failure modes and evaluation traps.** High classification accuracy can mean accepting nearly everything while missing rare logical errors. BP can converge confidently to the wrong sector. Confidence calibration can shift with p, basis, or decoder settings. Never expose truth, fallback outputs, or fallback runtime as deployment features.
+
+   Use separate sampling seeds for train/validation/test, splitting original shot pairs before augmentation or balancing. Keep all policies on identical held-out shots. Match the kit: pair X/Z failures with OR, sum their decode times, and report `1-(1-P_pair)^(1/12)`. Do not average basis rates or divide by 12 logical qubits.
+
+   Count light work, feature extraction, inference, and fallback in cascade timing. Report initialization separately, worker/thread counts, peak RSS, and throughput separately from per-pair service latency. Preserve slow shots and timeouts. Use paired uncertainty estimates and binomial bounds for sparse failures; the kit’s zero standard error at zero failures is misleading. A few hundred shots cannot establish Relay-level reliability or a stable p99. Eventually sweep all three p values.
+
+5. **Loss: defer it.** If the core result finishes early, use a separate, explicitly labeled heralded-erasure experiment at fixed cycle boundaries. `HERALDED_ERASE` models a flagged maximally mixed qubit, not persistent missing hardware; its DEM conversion approximates disjoint effects, so sample the circuit, not its DEM ([Stim documentation](https://github.com/quantumlib/Stim/blob/main/doc/gates.md#heralded_erase)). Correct insertion requires repairing measurement-record references, retaining herald locations, and conditioning decoder priors on them. Avoid blindly reusing `rescale_noise`: it rejects mixed probabilities, and effect merging loses location provenance needed for conditioning. Compare identical erasure shots with and without herald information. This is enough extra work to threaten the main deliverable.
+
+6. **First experiment.** At p=0.004, sample 512 X/Z pairs; split 256/128/128 for training/validation/test. Run BP-10, BP-20, unchanged Relay, and baseline BP-OSD on all shots; run OSD-0 only on BP-20 rejects. Use eight single-threaded workers. Based on kit timings, target comfortably under 15 minutes, checking throughput after 32 pairs; unfinished work is reported, not silently discarded.
+
+   Produce acceptance coverage, harmful substitutions, rescues, total mean latency, and descriptive tails for convergence-only and ML gates. Proceed if a gate saves substantial time without an obvious failure increase; retain the middle tier only if it improves that frontier. If ML sees too few failures to learn, retain the deterministic gate and collect more data. This pilot tests feasibility, not noninferiority.

